# Hierarchically Porous Metal–Organic Framework/MoS2 Interface for Selective Photocatalytic Conversion of CO2 with H2O into CH3COOH

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) provide a platform to design new heterogeneous catalysts for catalytic CO2 reduction, but selective formation of C2 valuable liquid fuel products remains a challenge. Herein, we propose a strategy to synthesize composites by integrating MoS2 nanosheets into hierarchically porous defective UiO-66 (d-UiO-66) to form Mo-O-Zr bimetallic sites on the interfaces between UiO-66 and MoS2 . The active interfaces are favorable for the efficient transfer of photo-generated charge carriers and for promoting the activity, whereas, the synergy of the components at the interfaces achieves selectivity for C2 production. The d-UiO-66/MoS2 composite facilitates the photo-catalytic conversion of gas phase CO2 and H2 O to CH3 COOH under visible light irradiation without any other adducts. The evolution rate and selectivity of CH3 COOH reached 39.0 μmol g-1 h-1 and 94 %, respectively, without any C1 products, suggesting a new approach for the design of highly efficient photocatalysts of CO2 for C2 production. Theoretical calculations demonstrate the charge-polarized Zr-O-Mo aided the C-C coupling process with the largely reduced energy barrier.

Hierarchically Porous Metal–Organic Framework/MoS2 Interface for Selective Photocatalytic Conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ into $\mathbf{CH_{3}C O O H}$
The increase in atmospheric carbon dioxide is one of the most pressing problems facing our planet. While each stage in the carbon cycle, capture, regeneration and conversion, has its own material requirements,[1] impressive metal-organic frameworks that contain both adsorption and catalyst sites with outstanding structural designability are emerging as a potential platform for the capture and subsequent conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into high value-added chemicals.[2] Inspired by natural photosynthesis, chemists have developed intriguing strategies to flexibly incorporate a photo-sensitizer, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and activation sites into one single metal-organic framework for the synthesis of fuel or chemical from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ using renewable solar energy.[3,4] Recent advances demonstrate that the enhanced photocatalytic performance of metal-organic framework/semiconductor hybrid materials enables the direct reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for C1 production (CO, HCOOH and $\mathrm{CH}_{4,}^{\cdot}$ ).[5–8] However, selective formation of valuable $^{C2+}$ products, such as ethanol and acetic acid, from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction remains markedly challenging, due to the difficulty of the $C^{-}C$ coupling process and the necessity of dual active catalytic sites,[9,10] for selective fabrication of $^{C2+}$ products.
Notably, hierarchically porous UiO-66 and its defective analogues were combined to create composite catalytic materials with nano-semiconductors and other nano-constituents for hydrogen evolution and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[11,12] We used flexible calcination methods to synthesize $d$ -UiO-66 with missing-linker defects, and an in situ synthetic strategy to fabricate composite $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction. We envisioned that the unsaturated Mo atoms in $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ sheets would rationally regulate contact interfaces with $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ nodes to form Mo-O-Zr bimetallic sites, enhancing the efficiency of the photogenerated charge carriers between the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets and $d$ -UiO-66 for the selective conversion of C2 production. By loading the $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ on a nylon 66 microfiltration membrane, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ was selectively formed as high as $39.0\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ under visible light irradiation without any sacrificial reagents, accomplishing a kinetically challenging 8-electron reaction: $2\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\mathbf{g})+$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{g})=\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}(1)+2\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{g})$ (Scheme 1). To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report of thin-film devices that enable overall selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction in gas-solid conditions to form $C2$ products free of hole scavengers.
By calcinating UiO-66 in Ar at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{h}$ , defective UiO-66 was obtained and characterized with PXRD, wherein, new peaks at low $2\theta$ (approximately $6^{\circ}$ ) were attributed to the (110) reflections of a primitive cubic phase of UiO- $66^{[13]}$ and the ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm confirmed that the hierarchical meso and micropores were maintained during calcination (Figure S1).[14] As schematically illustrated in Figure 1h, the hybrid material $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ was synthesized via a facile one-step hydrothermal method, and the structural morphology of the new material was characterized by SEM and TEM (Figure 1). $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ grew uniformly from the pores of $d$ -UiO-66 with a nanosheet structure (Figure 1a), and elemental mapping showed that Mo and S were distributed uniformly in the heterostructure. Three contents (mass ratio analyzed by EDX, XPS and ICP-AES are shown in Table S3), i.e., $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and $10\mathrm{{wt}\%}$ , were chosen for the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ loading, and the samples were named as $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\cdot$ - $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and $10\mathrm{\wt\%}$ . The BET surface areas of assynthesized $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2^{-}}2\mathrm{wt}\%$ , $d{\mathrm{-UiO{-}66/M o S_{2}{\cdot}5\ \mathrm{wt\\%}}}$ , $d\mathrm{-UiO{-}66/M o S_{2}\mathrm{{-}10\mathrm{{wt}\mathrm{{\%}}}}}$ are 354, 272, $56\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, implying that the cavities of the host framework are possibly occupied by highly dispersed $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure S2a). We estimate that defective UiO-66 was anchored to exposed $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets surface sites with smaller nanosheets buried homogeneously in the ordered defect nanoregions. The structure of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ was centrifuged from the liquor of $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ leached by hydrofluoric acid, the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets of $\approx100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ covered the surface of $d$ -UiO-66 (Figure S3a), and smaller $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets of $\approx30~\mathrm{nm}$ were fabricated in the defect nanoregions (Figure S3b). Additionally, the metallic phase $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ was separated from the dissociated $d$ -UiO-66/ $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure S4) according to Raman spectroscopy.
For the XPS spectra of Mo 3d and $\mathtt{S2p}$ in the bare $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ separated from the dissociated $d$ -UiO $){-}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure 2 c, d), deconvolution was carried out to separate $\mathrm{1T-MoS}_{2}$ and 2H$\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ emission peaks, and the results were consistent with those of previous reports.[21,22] Compared with those of pure $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets, the emission peaks of $\mathrm{Mo^{4+}}$ and $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ of 2H$\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ disappear, and the binding energies of $\mathbf{Mo^{4+}}$ 3d and $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ $2\mathrm{p}$ of $\mathrm{1T-MoS}_{2}$ are positively shifted by 0.29 and $0.1\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, in the $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ spectrum. Charge transfer from $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ to UiO-66 occurred after hybridization, according to the increase in electron density around the $\boldsymbol{z}_{\mathrm{r}}$ ions discussed above.[20] The ratio of the oxidized $\mathbf{Mo}^{6+}3\mathbf{d}_{3/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks located at 236.25 and $233.3\mathrm{eV}$ in the XPS spectra indicates a high percentage of Mo 3d after hybridization (Figure 2 c), consistent with the interface between $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and the $\boldsymbol{z}_{\mathrm{r}}$ oxide SBU.[23] The survey spectrum of the UiO- $66/$ $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ composite also indicates the presence of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure S6) similar to the $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ , the Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ peaks, Mo 3d peaks show a scarce shift to lower binding energies compared to those of the pristine UiO-66 and the bare $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , respectively (Figure 2 e, and f). The SEM of the nanocomposite UiO- $66/\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ (Figure S7) shows that the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets on the pristine UiO-66 external octahedral crystal surface are few and far between in proportion to the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets prepared by hydrothermal method. We infer that for the hybridized $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ in the defect nano-regions, the unsaturated Mo atoms of the exposed edge sites as the active sites of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ can rationally regulate the contact interface with the $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ nodes of UiO-66 to the Mo-O$Z\mathrm{r}$ .
Compared with pristine UiO-66, $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ plays a significant role in the light-absorption properties of the nanocomposites, leading to an obvious reduction in the band gap $\left(\mathrm{Eg}\right)$ from $3.70\mathrm{eV}$ for UiO-66 to $2.10\mathrm{eV}$ for $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure 3 a). In contrast to the UiO- ${\cdot66}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ that was synthesized by using pure UiO-66 with an $\mathrm{Eg}$ that was insignificantly changed by loading $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ on the surface, $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ has a lower band energy, is sensitive to visible light, and can be excited to produce active carriers under visible light (Figure S8),[24] wherein the chemical interaction between $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66 favors the separation of photo-generated electrons and holes. In the Mott-Schottky plots (Figure 3 b), the apparent electron carrier concentrations were inversely proportional to the slope.[25] According to the slope of the M-S plots (Figure 3 b), the electron carrier concentrations of $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ are approx. 5 and 8 times, respectively, that of the normal Ui ${\bf O}{-}66/{\bf M}{\bf o}{\bf S}_{2}$ and UiO-66, indicating that $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ doping inside the pores is beneficial for increasing the concentration of excess free electrons. Figure 3 c shows the clear EIS pattern of the photocatalysts and the corresponding fitting circuit, the values of Rs and R decrease after the combination of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66. The resistance of the catalysts weakened (Table S4), possibly due to the efficient interfaces between the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66 of the $d$ -UiO-66/ $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ that facilitated the transfer of carriers; and these interfaces exhibited the weakest interfacial resistance compared with those of UiO- ${\cdot66}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ . The highest photocurrent density was observed in the case of $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ in comparison with that in UiO- $66/\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ and UiO-66 (Figure $3\mathrm{d}$ ), indicating that the heterojunction and the active interfaces are propitious for the efficient separation of photogenerated $\operatorname{e-h}^{+}$ pairs and charge transfer.
The photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor on the $d$ -UiO- ${\cdot66}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ photocatalysts was investigated under visible light irradiation $(\lambda\geq400\mathrm{nm})$ . Using NMR and GC, $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ was determined as the major carbonous product with a trace amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ $(2.5\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmathbf{g}^{-1}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ as a side product without any C1 products (CO, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ ) (Figure S9 & S10). The optimized $d$ - $\mathrm{JiO-66/MoS}_{2}{\cdot}5\mathrm{wt}^{\mathrm{\circ}},$ % photocatalyst shows $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ evolution of $39.0\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmathrm{g}^{-1}\up h^{-1}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ evolution of $81\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure 4a). Notably, the achieved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-C2 photocatalysis conversion rate is the highest compared to photocatalysts in the other reported works (Table S2). To verify that the interaction at the interface plays a leading role in the selectivity of the products, UiO- ${\cdot66}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ was synthesized following the same procedure as $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}5\mathrm{wt}\%$ (Figure S7). The HCOOH produced by the photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was generated over $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure 4 c), and the yield was consistent with $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs previously reported, such as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - UiO-66,[26] CdS/UiO-66,[27] $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/U i O-66,}^{\prime}$ [28] UiO- $66/C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}[29]$ and $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S@UiO-66-NH}_{2}$ .[30] While UiO-66 has a large band gap that can only adsorb UV light, trace HCOOH was detected over $d$ -UiO-66 with missing linker defects that lowered the ligand-to-metal charge transfer energy gaps and increased the charge transfer likelihood, as previously reports.[31] These results demonstrate that the formation of an interface between $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66 promoted the transfer of photoinduced carriers to enhance the evolution of products, and synergistic catalysis at the interface between $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66 enhanced the evolution of C2 products.
Constant $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ evolution within at least 4 cycles during a total of $160\mathrm{h}$ of reaction implies the high stability. The molar ratio of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is close to 1:2, suggesting that the stoichiometric reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , was reliably achieved on $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ photocatalyst under visible light irradiation (Figure 4b). Similarly, the SEM image and XRD pattern of $d{\mathrm{-UiO-}}66/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ after $160\mathrm{h}$ of photocatalysis show that the catalyst exhibited a highly stable performance for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Figure S13). The control experiments show that no organic substance was present in the reaction solution before or after light irradiation while Ar was bubbled into the solution (Figure S14). When isotopic $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ gas was used as the reactant, the liquid and gas products obtained after $40\mathrm{{h}}$ of irradiation showed two peaks at 177.22 and $21.03\mathrm{ppm}$ corresponding to $^{13}{\mathrm{CH}}_{3}^{13}{\mathrm{COOH}}$ in the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR and two split methyl proton resonances appeared in the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum due to a one-bond coupling between $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ (Figure S15). No signal could be detected in the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectrum when $^{12}{\bf C O}_{2}$ was employed as the reactant.
According to the Mott-Schottky plots (Figure S16), the positive slope of the plot indicates the n-type nature of the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66, of which Ef and Eb are determined to be $-0.58{\mathrm{V}}$ and $-0.40\mathrm{V}$ (vs. NHE), respectively. The CBMs of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66, which are estimated to be $-0.68\mathrm{V}$ and $-0.50{\mathrm{V}}^{[32]}$ (vs. NHE), is more negative than the redox potentials of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ( $-0.31\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ vs. NHE). The Tauc plot (Figure 3a) estimates that the band gap of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ is $1.8\mathrm{eV},$ consistent with the finding reported for $\mathrm{1T-MoS}_{2}$ ,[33] and the band gap of defective UiO-66 with missing linkers is $3.70\mathrm{eV},$ which is slightly lower than that of defect-free UiO-66 (3.8– $4.1\mathrm{eV}$ ). Consequently, the VBMs of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and UiO-66, which are calculated to be $+1.12$ and $+3.2\mathrm{V}$ (vs. NHE), respectively, are more positive than the redox potential of $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(+\ 0.82\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE). The CBM and VBM positions are staggered between UiO-66 and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , and the nanojunction formed between UiO-66 and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ thus results in efficient electron transfer through the Mo-O- $Z\mathrm{r}$ interface within the hybrid structure. As $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ has a higher conduction band of approximately $0.18\mathrm{eV}$ than UiO-66, $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ is expected to provide a stronger driving force for the transfer of electrons to UiO-66, from which $Z\mathbf{r}^{3+}$ ions were generated and characterized by electron spin resonance (EPR) spectroscopy (Figure S17). Dual metal sites with asymmetric charge distributions may considerably lower the $C^{\mathrm{-}}C$ coupling energy barrier, facilitating the formation of the desirable C2 products.[9] Additionally, the holes remaining in the VB of the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ have higher hole mobility and transfer quickly towards the surface of the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ ,[34] achieving water oxidation on the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ surface.
